WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.146
Welcome to the Federal Modernization
Workbook's developer workbook,

2
00:00:04.146 --> 00:00:05.912
Building Non-Person Identity.

3
00:00:05.912 --> 00:00:09.812
This is Chapter 7: least
privilege for service principals.

4
00:00:09.812 --> 00:00:14.989
A permission nobody reviews stays forever,
so this chapter's sweep reads every

5
00:00:14.989 --> 00:00:20.087
grant, asks its owner once a quarter, and
removes only what a reviewer approved.

6
00:00:20.087 --> 00:00:23.610
We'll start from Learn's own
review-and-revoke script,

7
00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:28.199
see why it shouldn't be run as published,
then walk the sweep, the rules,

8
00:00:28.199 --> 00:00:32.787
the owner review, the plan and its
write path, two labs and the dangers.

9
00:00:32.787 --> 00:00:38.290
One note before we begin: the code is
illustrative, tested against fixtures.

10
00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:43.978
Start with what we're actually measuring.

11
00:00:43.978 --> 00:00:48.026
An app can hold two kinds of
permission: an application permission,

12
00:00:48.026 --> 00:00:51.559
which is a role it holds on
another app's service principal,

13
00:00:51.559 --> 00:00:56.416
and a delegated permission, which is a
grant to act for one person or for everyone.

14
00:00:56.416 --> 00:00:59.916
Learn's way to see what an app
can reach is to walk every service

15
00:00:59.916 --> 00:01:04.103
principal's role assignments, paying
close attention to Microsoft Graph.

16
00:01:04.103 --> 00:01:08.278
And remember that directory roles
and mail roles grant access too.

17
00:01:08.278 --> 00:01:12.860
For this estate, everything goes to the
worldwide Graph and commercial Azure,

18
00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:16.800
and every call is available in
the government clouds as well.

19
00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:24.675
The program adds its rules on
top, marked as design guidance.

20
00:01:24.675 --> 00:01:27.162
Owners review every grant each quarter.

21
00:01:27.162 --> 00:01:32.274
No broad tenant-wide permission stands
without a written, dated justification.

22
00:01:32.274 --> 00:01:36.432
Where Learn documents a narrower
replacement, like selected sites,

23
00:01:36.432 --> 00:01:38.807
or scoped mail access, that's the target,

24
00:01:38.807 --> 00:01:43.113
and for mail the old unscoped grant
has to go or the scope means nothing.

25
00:01:43.113 --> 00:01:47.413
Managed identities get Azure roles at
the resource, not the subscription.

26
00:01:47.413 --> 00:01:51.250
Privileged service principals belong
to the Privileged Access plan.

27
00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:54.450
And the code removes
a grant, never an app.

28
00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:00.075
Here's Microsoft's starting point.

29
00:02:00.075 --> 00:02:03.892
Learn's review page has a PowerShell
script that finds one app,

30
00:02:03.892 --> 00:02:07.634
removes all of its delegated
grants, then tries to remove all

31
00:02:07.634 --> 00:02:11.750
of its application permissions,
plus the matching Graph requests.

32
00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:14.892
A separate tutorial shows
how to grant and revoke,

33
00:02:14.892 --> 00:02:18.188
which the second lab uses
to restore what it removes.

34
00:02:18.188 --> 00:02:20.856
As in earlier chapters, the page is pinned

35
00:02:20.856 --> 00:02:24.151
unmodified with checked
hashes and a weekly watch.

36
00:02:24.151 --> 00:02:27.725
The harness then runs the
script exactly as published,

37
00:02:27.725 --> 00:02:31.300
against stand-ins that follow
Learn's own API contract.

38
00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:34.160
That contract is where
things get interesting.

39
00:02:37.910 --> 00:02:41.323
Run as published, the script
has two opposite problems.

40
00:02:41.323 --> 00:02:43.034
First, it's indiscriminate:

41
00:02:43.034 --> 00:02:47.859
it removes every delegated grant and
tries every application permission,

42
00:02:47.859 --> 00:02:50.427
with no list of what to
keep and no dry run,

43
00:02:50.427 --> 00:02:54.785
so a permission someone formally
approved goes along with the rest.

44
00:02:54.785 --> 00:02:57.093
Second, on Learn's own API contract,

45
00:02:57.093 --> 00:03:02.368
its application permission removals miss
entirely: it passes the app's own ID

46
00:03:02.368 --> 00:03:07.560
where the reference says the resource
belongs, so all three answer not found.

47
00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:10.300
The code keeps the requests as its model,

48
00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:13.990
and adds the keep list,
the decisions and the plan.

49
00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:20.665
The rest of the gaps are
about scope and privilege.

50
00:03:20.665 --> 00:03:25.353
To find one app's grants, the script
reads every grant in the tenant.

51
00:03:25.353 --> 00:03:28.208
To review, it signs in
with write permissions,

52
00:03:28.208 --> 00:03:32.839
including the very broad directory
one this chapter is trying to remove.

53
00:03:32.839 --> 00:03:36.853
It names a different administrator
role than the API reference does.

54
00:03:36.853 --> 00:03:39.141
It hard-codes the worldwide host.

55
00:03:39.141 --> 00:03:43.275
And it never looks at directory
roles or Azure role assignments,

56
00:03:43.275 --> 00:03:45.069
so an app in a directory role,

57
00:03:45.069 --> 00:03:49.828
or a managed identity that's Contributor
on a subscription, passes unseen.

58
00:03:49.828 --> 00:03:53.560
So the verdict: use the
requests, not the script.

59
00:03:57.310 --> 00:03:58.535
What does the code add?

60
00:03:58.535 --> 00:04:02.863
A module, two entry scripts, one
that only reads and one that writes,

61
00:04:02.863 --> 00:04:06.790
its own call list, and a reviewed
list of what counts as broad,

62
00:04:06.790 --> 00:04:09.435
with Learn's replacement for each class.

63
00:04:09.435 --> 00:04:14.578
Three small files carry the human decisions:
exceptions from the security office,

64
00:04:14.578 --> 00:04:18.360
the owners' quarterly answers,
and the Azure scopes to look at.

65
00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:22.450
The sweep then reads every
service principal in the register:

66
00:04:22.450 --> 00:04:26.701
its roles on every resource, not
just Graph, its delegated grants,

67
00:04:26.701 --> 00:04:30.872
its directory roles, and for a
managed identity, its Azure roles.

68
00:04:30.872 --> 00:04:33.250
Every grant gets a stable key.

69
00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:39.725
The check compares the
sweep with the reviewed

70
00:04:39.725 --> 00:04:42.450
list and the exceptions,
and applies nine rules.

71
00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:46.945
The heart of it: a broad permission with
no current, complete exception is a

72
00:04:46.945 --> 00:04:49.892
finding, whether it's an
application permission

73
00:04:49.892 --> 00:04:52.250
or a delegated one granted to everyone.

74
00:04:52.250 --> 00:04:57.347
Two rules cover mail, including a subtle
one: if the exception relies on scoped

75
00:04:57.347 --> 00:05:02.125
mail access but the unscoped grant is
still there, the scope does nothing.

76
00:05:02.125 --> 00:05:06.662
Two more grade managed identities'
Azure roles by how wide the scope is.

77
00:05:06.662 --> 00:05:10.710
Privileged apps are only noted
here, because they're handed off.

78
00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:17.235
Two pieces sit between
the check and the plan.

79
00:05:17.235 --> 00:05:21.046
First, the escalation class:
a handful of permissions

80
00:05:21.046 --> 00:05:24.510
that let an app grant itself
more, or act as others.

81
00:05:24.510 --> 00:05:28.819
Any app holding one is handed off
whole to the Privileged Access plan,

82
00:05:28.819 --> 00:05:30.860
with everything it holds listed.

83
00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:34.397
Second, the quarterly owner
review: one row per grant,

84
00:05:34.397 --> 00:05:38.510
sent to the owners, and only
answers dated this quarter count.

85
00:05:38.510 --> 00:05:40.876
It closes when every row is decided and

86
00:05:40.876 --> 00:05:43.760
nothing approved for
removal is still granted.

87
00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:47.504
Access reviews for privileged apps
belong to the other workbook,

88
00:05:47.504 --> 00:05:50.090
and the chapter treats them as preview.

89
00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:57.713
The plan turns the owners'
answers into one action each,

90
00:05:57.713 --> 00:05:59.778
and only two kinds ever write.

91
00:05:59.778 --> 00:06:03.909
Removing an application permission
happens through the resource,

92
00:06:03.909 --> 00:06:08.189
and only once any replacement, like
selected sites, is already in place.

93
00:06:08.189 --> 00:06:12.515
Narrowing a delegated grant can
only make it smaller, never empty.

94
00:06:12.515 --> 00:06:16.906
Everything else is recorded: waiting
for a decision or a replacement,

95
00:06:16.906 --> 00:06:21.297
kept under an exception, or handed
to someone else's reviewed process,

96
00:06:21.297 --> 00:06:24.840
such as re-scoping an Azure
role in infrastructure code.

97
00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:30.980
As before, identical inputs give an identical
file, so a reviewer approves one hash.

98
00:06:34.730 --> 00:06:39.030
As in the last chapter, writing is a
separate script with strict refusals.

99
00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:41.232
The plan must be the approved one,

100
00:06:41.232 --> 00:06:44.493
for this tenant and cloud,
and less than a week old.

101
00:06:44.493 --> 00:06:48.921
It can only delete one kind of thing,
a role assignment through its resource,

102
00:06:48.921 --> 00:06:51.279
and it can only shrink a delegated grant.

103
00:06:51.279 --> 00:06:55.305
And it runs as a person, signed in
with the right administrator role.

104
00:06:55.305 --> 00:06:56.367
Why not an app?

105
00:06:56.367 --> 00:06:58.997
Because the permissions
it needs are exactly

106
00:06:58.997 --> 00:07:01.556
the escalation permissions
the sweep flags.

107
00:07:01.556 --> 00:07:06.400
Before each write it re-reads the
target, and skips anything that changed.

108
00:07:10.150 --> 00:07:11.200
Who's involved?

109
00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:13.037
Developers build the tooling.

110
00:07:13.037 --> 00:07:16.163
Application owners answer
the quarterly review.

111
00:07:16.163 --> 00:07:19.984
The identity governance officer
closes it and receives the hand-off

112
00:07:19.984 --> 00:07:23.337
of privileged apps, and the
security officers check the gate.

113
00:07:23.337 --> 00:07:25.700
The permission split mirrors the code.

114
00:07:25.700 --> 00:07:28.151
The sweep holds three read permissions in

115
00:07:28.151 --> 00:07:30.812
Graph and Reader in Azure,
nothing that writes.

116
00:07:30.812 --> 00:07:35.533
The write task holds the two grant-management
permissions and the administrator

117
00:07:35.533 --> 00:07:38.032
role, but only in a
person's signed-in session,

118
00:07:38.032 --> 00:07:40.830
so nothing standing can grant itself more.

119
00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:48.755
Lab 1 runs on a laptop with
Chapter 1's inventory in place.

120
00:07:48.755 --> 00:07:51.239
First, Learn's script
against the stand-ins:

121
00:07:51.239 --> 00:07:56.136
read the test where all three application
permission removals come back not found,

122
00:07:56.136 --> 00:07:59.330
because the script named
the wrong service principal.

123
00:07:59.330 --> 00:08:04.093
Then list the permissions each task
needs, which match the table we just saw.

124
00:08:04.093 --> 00:08:08.645
Then run the sweep over a fictional
estate: eleven service principals,

125
00:08:08.645 --> 00:08:12.413
twenty grants, three handed to
the Privileged Access plan,

126
00:08:12.413 --> 00:08:15.004
and an owner review that isn't closed yet.

127
00:08:15.004 --> 00:08:19.100
Two agent identities are skipped,
because they're Chapter 9's.

128
00:08:22.850 --> 00:08:27.212
Now read the findings, and each
rule shows up in a realistic shape.

129
00:08:27.212 --> 00:08:31.824
A mail automation app holds the broad
directory permission with no exception.

130
00:08:31.824 --> 00:08:34.988
A connector's exception
quietly ended last month.

131
00:08:34.988 --> 00:08:37.546
A mail permission is "scoped" in Exchange,

132
00:08:37.546 --> 00:08:41.576
but the unscoped grant is still
there, so the scope does nothing.

133
00:08:41.576 --> 00:08:44.069
A batch machine's managed
identity holds roles

134
00:08:44.069 --> 00:08:46.500
at the subscription and
the management group.

135
00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:49.975
Three apps can grant themselves
more, so they're handed off.

136
00:08:49.975 --> 00:08:53.720
And in the review, one answer
from July doesn't count any more,

137
00:08:53.720 --> 00:08:55.690
because it's a new quarter.

138
00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:00.378
Then the plan.

139
00:09:00.378 --> 00:09:02.208
Of eleven actions, three write:

140
00:09:02.208 --> 00:09:07.186
a connector's full-control site permission
is removed because its selected-sites

141
00:09:07.186 --> 00:09:11.797
replacement is already granted, the
broad directory permission is removed,

142
00:09:11.797 --> 00:09:16.116
and a partner app's all-users grant
is narrowed to just reading profiles.

143
00:09:16.116 --> 00:09:20.328
The dry run shows the point of the
whole exercise: both removals go

144
00:09:20.328 --> 00:09:24.853
through Microsoft Graph's service
principal, the resource, not the app.

145
00:09:24.853 --> 00:09:29.990
Applying it to the fake sends three writes,
and a hash nobody approved is refused.

146
00:09:29.990 --> 00:09:34.710
Delete the output afterwards,
because the review names real owners.

147
00:09:38.460 --> 00:09:43.910
Lab 2 uses a test tenant and subscription,
never production, with fictional people.

148
00:09:43.910 --> 00:09:48.416
You create a broad grant and an
over-scoped managed identity on purpose,

149
00:09:48.416 --> 00:09:52.998
then sweep with read-only permissions
and confirm both findings appear.

150
00:09:52.998 --> 00:09:55.302
An owner decision of remove goes in,

151
00:09:55.302 --> 00:09:59.673
a second person approves the plan's
hash, and you apply it as a person.

152
00:09:59.673 --> 00:10:02.641
You also try the script's
original request shape once,

153
00:10:02.641 --> 00:10:04.935
and write down what Graph really answers.

154
00:10:04.935 --> 00:10:06.250
After a few minutes,

155
00:10:06.250 --> 00:10:10.360
the grant is gone and the audit
log shows a person as the actor.

156
00:10:10.360 --> 00:10:12.190
Then restore everything.

157
00:10:15.940 --> 00:10:18.215
Each danger here has a guard in the code.

158
00:10:18.215 --> 00:10:22.256
The most direct is simply running
Learn's script against a real tenant,

159
00:10:22.256 --> 00:10:24.765
which removes far more
than anyone planned.

160
00:10:24.765 --> 00:10:29.648
Close behind are deleting an app instead of
a grant, and removing a broad permission

161
00:10:29.648 --> 00:10:34.177
before its replacement is in place, which
takes a workload down in production.

162
00:10:34.177 --> 00:10:38.845
Then the subtle ones: a mail scope that
does nothing while the old grant stays,

163
00:10:38.845 --> 00:10:43.808
a narrowing that actually widens, last
quarter's answer passing as this quarter's,

164
00:10:43.808 --> 00:10:47.580
and a permission quietly
re-granted at the next consent.

165
00:10:51.330 --> 00:10:52.680
Here's what done looks like.

166
00:10:52.680 --> 00:10:56.193
Every service principal was
swept, or skipped with a reason.

167
00:10:56.193 --> 00:10:58.577
Every broad permission has a current,

168
00:10:58.577 --> 00:11:02.117
complete exception or a decision
to remove or narrow it.

169
00:11:02.117 --> 00:11:05.470
No mail scope is pretending
while the old grant stays,

170
00:11:05.470 --> 00:11:09.280
and every managed identity's
Azure role sits at the resource.

171
00:11:09.280 --> 00:11:13.468
The privileged apps went to the Privileged
Access plan and were closed there.

172
00:11:13.468 --> 00:11:15.450
The quarter's review is closed,

173
00:11:15.450 --> 00:11:19.493
and every write ran as a person,
on a plan someone else approved.

174
00:11:19.493 --> 00:11:23.755
Next, Chapter 8 puts Conditional
Access on workload identities.

175
00:11:23.755 --> 00:11:24.900
Thanks for watching.
